\par Wu looked up from his ClipCom and watched as she walked across the crowded lab and slumped into a swivel chair.  His eyebrows went up, questioningly.  "Well?"
\par 
\par She looked at him and nodded.  "We won.  We'll start making preparations tomorrow."
\par 
\par "Why so glum, then?"
\par 
\par "Because we have a very minimal allocation of resources.  Because we'll probably have to cut corners, and that will make a potentially dangerous job even more dangerous.  Because I have no idea what we're up against, or even how to investigate it, much le
ss fight it."
\par 
\par "Your friend Moon would know."
\par 
\par She sighed.  Wu was the only person in Plymouth with whom she'd shared knowledge of her communications with Axen.  She'd thought sharing that with a trusted associate would make things easier.  Sometimes it only made them more difficult.
\par 
\par She glanced at the equipment rack in the corner where Frost was installed, silent and dark, perhaps dreaming again as she \emdash  it \emdash  so often did these days.  "Frost?"
\par 
\par The computer was slow to respond, but Emma crossed her arms over her chest and waited patiently.
\par 
\par "Yes, Emma."
\par 
\par "Please try to open a communication link to Axen."
\par 
\par Another pause.  "No response.  The control channels we have used for communication are still blocked by jamming from Eden.  I have been trying to establish a link every two hours since the satellite station became operational."
\par 
\par "I know, Frost, thank you.  Please keep trying."
\par 
\par "Emma."
\par 
\par "Yes?"
\par 
\par "I have an anomalous reading on the satellite.  Would you like to hear about it?"
\par 
\par "Please."
\par 
\par "This lab is equipped with a small telescope, and I have been observing the optical characteristics of the satellite.  Its brightness is changing in what can only be described as an artificial and purposeful manner."
\par 
\par Wu rubbed his forehead and stared at the computer.  "You're saying it\rquote s flashing or something?"
\par 
\par "The light appears to be reflected from an external source, possibly on the ground.  The spectra are similar to that of the observed Eden Laser weapon."
\par 
\par Emma's mouth opened wordlessly, and she started to smile.  If the satellite were jammed by acciden
t, and if one had access to a powerful weapons laser, this would be another way of using the satellite to get a message through.  "Can you decode the signal?"
\par 
\par "It seems to be a compressed, burst-mode, audio signal.  The message repeats at random intervals with gaps of several hours to several days.  I have recorded it, however, and can play it for you."
\par 
\par "Go ahead." Axen, that crafty old fox, had found a way to get through.
\par 
\par An audio tone announced the start of the playback.  It was a man's voice, but it w
as definitely not Axen.  "This is a friend from Eden.  My name is Eldon Jensen and I'm a scientist in Eden working on various defense projects.  The secrets of what happened are in Eden.  Beware contamination.  Go in at night.  What you want is in the Adv
anced Labs.  I'll send again when I can."
\par 
\par "End of message," announced Frost.
\par 
\par Contamination? Night? This made less sense all the time.
\par 
\par Wu made eye contact.  "It could be a trick."
\par 
\par Emma nodded.  "He certainly could have provided more useful information."
\par 
\par "The message," said Frost, "represents a grossly inefficient use of the limited bandwidth.  Text only would have been thousands of times more efficient."
\par 
\par Emma's fingers drummed on a lab bench as she thought.  "But he might suppose that a personalized voice message would be more likely to seem sincere, or more plausibly, he didn't think at all.  He's a man with a guilty conscience who took a chance opportun
ity to send a message, no planning or forethought, certainly not the instincts of a trained spy."
\par 
\par Wu rubbed the tip of his nose.  "Then you think it's genuine?"
\par 
\par "I'm inclined to for the moment.  Let\rquote s just hope that our friend in Eden isn't such a neophyte that he gets caught before he can provide us with some really useful information.  At any rate, it tells us more about what we should be looking for."

\par 
\par One thing was for sure, her planned expedition was only the beginning.  She whistled quietly to herself.  "I was just thinking of a phrase I once read," she said.
\par 
\par Wu cocked his head.  "What?"
\par 
\par "Though I walk through the valley of the shadow of death..."
